WEBVTT

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:11.760
This is Izakaya Nobu.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.490
It's an ordinary izakaya, but 
there is one extraordinary thing.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:14.990
<b>Nobuyuki Yazawa (32)
 Owner of the izakaya "Nobu"</b>

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.600
Our front door opens into 
a parallel universe!

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.000
<b>Shinobu Senke (23)
 Waitress at the izakaya "Nobu"</b>

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.010
Isekai Izakaya

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.010
Japanese Food From Another World

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:33.010
<b>A Tempura Early Summer Mix</b>

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:34.510
<b>Izakaya Nobu</b>

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:35.910
<b>Lorenz (50)
Glass Craft Guild Meister</b>

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:35.920
Heya!

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:37.340
Welcome!

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:38.140
Welcome.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.650
I brought the thing that 
my son asked me for.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.310
Your son? You mean Hans-san?

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:44.210
Yeah.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:45.280
Oh.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.650
Are these beer steins?

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:49.270
Wow.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:50.930
These are fancy.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:52.450
So pretty.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:56.030
<b>Hermina (16)
Waitress at Nobu.
Berthold's wife</b>

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:53.880
They really are.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.030
These could be from a Dresden glass artist.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:57.730
What's that?

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:00.040
Oh, nothing. I was just talking to myself.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:01.170
That Hans...

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:05.440
What should I do, Dad?!  
I broke a stein at Nobu!

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:05.310
<b>Hans (23)
Palace Guard
Ancient City Aitheria</b>

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.260
Or so he said, with tears in his eyes.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:09.640
So I told him...

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.600
Anything that has a form 
will eventually break.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:15.930
If there is a beginning, there is an end.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:17.850
But it was only one that broke.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.980
Well, the other one is to apologize.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.760
If you have a debt, make 
sure to repay it double.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.130
That's how men do it.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.410
I'm sorry. I feel like you've 
gone out of your way for this.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:32.880
It's fine. But I'll have one, too.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.140
Right away!

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.740
Prosit!

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.810
Those guests are really enjoying themselves.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:39.510
Yeah.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:42.350
I wonder if they are celebrating something.

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:46.510
Oh, that's the old man from 
"Bears in the Flower Garden"!

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:48.400
You know him?

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.120
It's a place that's been around 
since I was an apprentice.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.060
They have great mead.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:54.150
Mead?

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.970
It's alcohol made with honey.

00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:01.030
<b>Droop</b>

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:59.860
They say it's the world's 
oldest alcoholic beverage.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:01.020
Huh.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.130
The place is famous for never 
having been closed a single day

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:05.960
ever since they first opened.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.920
I've been there quite a few times, too.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.630
Not even a single day?

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.170
That is impressive.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:16.680
I wonder what's going on today. 
I guess I'll go talk to them.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.290
Pops, this is Taishou and Shinobu-chan.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:29.350
Oh, nice to meet you. My name's Björn.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.560
<b>Björn
 Owner of "Bears in the Flower Garden"</b>

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:33.300
It's a real surprise to 
see you at Nobu though.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.960
Yeah, sorry for the noise.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:39.200
Everybody's come here because I'm retiring.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:40.360
What?!

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:41.730
Even though I didn't ask anyone to do this.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:43.110
You're retiring?!

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.080
<b>Fizz</b>

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:50.160
I've been using every trick in the 
book since I hurt my hip last year,

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.050
but it seems it's finally 
the end of the road for me.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:55.490
Pops...

00:02:55.940 --> 00:03:01.830
Besides, at this rate, my grandson is going 
to grow up without knowing who I am.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:07.060
My son's got a job that pays 
way better than running a bar,

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:09.850
and the bar's starting to 
show its age all over, too.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:14.670
There are no reasons to continue, 
but a thousand reasons to quit.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.770
I see.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.070
I know!

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:21.770
Taishou, please make a dish to celebrate.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:26.190
Something super auspicious. I mean, this 
is going to be a new beginning for me.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.580
I'll leave it up to you.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:30.550
Understood.

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:34.850
But this place is really nice.

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:36.640
It reminds me of my place!

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.630
You need to meet certain 
criteria for a good bar.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:42.500
Good drink and fish, sure,

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.280
but there's something really 
important besides that, too.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.010
Something really important?

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:50.000
I think this place already has it.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.000
I mean, it is on the same level as my place!

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.080
What are you talking about, Björn?

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:58.740
You don't serve anything as 
good as this at your place!

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:02.770
You don't get to say that after you've 
been coming to me for decades!

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:06.890
<b>Sizzle</b>

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:06.890
<b>Sizzle</b>

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:12.030
<b>Sizzle</b>

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:09.790
Oh, it's starting to sound good.

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:12.040
I wonder what this is going to be!

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:15.650
All right, it's done.

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:18.670
<b>Tempura Early Summer Mix</b>

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:18.670
<b>The crispy and delicious tempura mix 
is a perfect companion for beer!</b>

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:18.670
It's a tempura early summer mix.

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:21.680
This is the dip, and this is the salt.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:23.660
Please use whichever one you prefer.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:25.920
Wow, that looks good!

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.520
Björn, let me have some, too!

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.170
Wait, let Björn have some first!

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:32.820
<b>Crunch</b>

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.320
Taishou, are these fritters?

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.470
It's something like that.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:43.020
With tempura, when you make the batter puff 
like this, it's called making it bloom.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:45.830
I heard you were having 
a new beginning today.

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:46.900
Bloom?

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:53.370
I get it. So these are your flowers for me.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:54.160
Yes.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.550
I see. What a considerate dish.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:01.400
I happily accept your flowers, then.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:06.550
<b>Crunch</b>

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.410
Wow, this is...

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:15.170
The crunchy and supple textures 
are colliding in my mouth.

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:17.060
I've never experienced 
anything like this before!

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.700
Now I'll try it with this salt.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:27.170
Nice! This is a nice, unobtrusive flavor.

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:30.910
Is this Spargel?

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:32.570
Yes, it's asparagus.

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:36.450
I heard that here, Spargel is an auspicious 
sign that heralds the arrival of spring.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:38.580
That's correct.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:41.080
I recommend trying it with this salt.

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:43.200
What's making it green?

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:46.830
It's matcha... I mean, it's like an herb.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.960
Huh, interesting.

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:51.060
<b>Crunch</b>

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:54.380
Yes, yes.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:58.740
The herb's aroma and bitterness 
is indescribable.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:01.260
The different variations 
keep surprising me!

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:03.720
I am honored that you're enjoying it.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:06.030
I-I want some, too!

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:08.790
Taishou, please make some for us, too!

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:11.860
Understood. But before that...

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:16.720
What is this?

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:19.640
I made a clear soup with the pike conger 
that you had as tempura before.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:20.090
<b>Clear Broth of Pike Conger</b>

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:20.090
<b>A skillful chef can make the pike conger bloom 
in this soup. Enjoy this sophisticated flavor.</b>

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:21.180
Pike conger?

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.260
It's a type of eel.

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:25.290
The flavor's excellent.

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:29.320
Oh, there's a flower 
blooming in here as well.

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:31.370
Those are called hamo peonies.

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:32.690
Hamo peonies?

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:38.130
Pike conger, or hamo, have a lot 
of bones, so to prepare them,

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:41.250
you need to do a step called bone cutting 
where you sever the small bones.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:43.930
When you parboil the meat of the pike conger

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:48.160
it curls up beautifully
and looks just a peony.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:50.290
That's why these are called hamo peonies.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:06.070
I'm relieved... because there's 
a place like this in Aitheria.

00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:07.820
Björn-san...

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:10.590
That's it! Let's all come here now.

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:14.230
I'll keep you guys company 
as a guest next time!

00:07:14.230 --> 00:07:15.240
That sounds good.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:18.290
There's way better food 
here than your place, too!

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:21.350
We'll party each night with 
good drink and good food!

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:28.460
But it's quite sad that we won't be able 
to drink that place's mead anymore...

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:29.210
Yeah.

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:33.720
We always gathered there when 
something had happened.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:36.620
No, we gathered there even 
when nothing was up.

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:38.500
That's true.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:42.240
Then it didn't take much 
to start talking nonsense.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:44.140
Yeah, that's how it was...

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:47.240
I can't believe that place is going away.

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:52.240
I had fun every day these last decades.

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:54.690
We didn't have that many different drinks.

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:56.790
The fish wasn't particularly good.

00:07:57.140 --> 00:08:02.050
The reason my place could last this long 
despite all that was thanks to you.

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:05.610
Thank you. Really, thank you.

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:10.560
We will never forget your place!

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:15.310
Hey, Taishou, that stein from before...

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:18.700
If pops is going to be coming here...

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:23.200
I understand. We will make that 
Björn-san's designated stein.

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:29.160
Pops, those flowers are from me.

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:36.080
You used to be just a little brat.
Now you're making amazing things like this.

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:40.470
Thank you for this.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:46.900
Prosit!

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:49.610
Prosit!

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:51.830
Let's toast again as well!

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:53.720
Two draft beers for you!

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:56.110
Excellent timing!

00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:57.620
Let's go!

00:08:57.620 --> 00:08:59.720
Prosit!

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:07.730
I'm going to go home now. Good night!

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:09.260
Good night.

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:12.000
You're alone? Where is Berthold-san?

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:14.350
He's waiting at the next corner.

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:17.610
Apparently he's too embarrassed 
to wait in front of here.

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:19.300
Ah, I see.

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:21.540
I'm off then.

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:23.000
Be careful!

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.290
Shinobu-chan, I saved some 
pike conger soup for you.

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:28.800
You should have it before you go.

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:30.960
Thank you, Taishou.

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:34.200
Thanks for the food.

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:43.330
Yes, that's good.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.910
I wonder if he didn't really want to quit.

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:53.960
I felt like his back was saying that 
he wanted to keep going a bit longer.

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:57.090
He was trying hard to find 
reasons to quit, huh?

00:09:57.090 --> 00:09:57.970
Yes.

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:03.290
I guess he couldn't quit without 
finding all those reasons.

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:06.740
But he was able to decide 
when to quit himself.

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:09.310
I think that's admirable.

00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:12.640
<i>You need to meet certain 
criteria for a good bar.</i>

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:16.100
<i>Good drink and fish, sure,</i>

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:18.920
<i>but there's something really 
important besides that, too.</i>

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:25.570
<i>I wonder what the criteria 
for a good bar are...</i>

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:31.280
Next up, we have Nobu Plus.

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:33.020
<b>Nobu</b>

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.030
<b>Nobu</b>

00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:40.030
<b>Narrator
 Teiyu Ichiryusai</b>

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:40.040
Getting the season's flavors into one dish! 
You can make tempura in a frying pan!

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:45.730
Ryuuta Kijima is going to put his own 
spin on the dishes featured in Nobu!

00:10:43.300 --> 00:10:47.040
<b>Ryuuta Kijima Creates Nobu Dishes</b>

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:51.190
Tempura when you're asked to make something 
really delicious to celebrate retiring.

00:10:47.270 --> 00:10:51.170
<b>Ryuuta Kijima</b>

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:51.170
<b>Grew up in a household where both his grandmother and his mother were culinary researchers,
so he got into cooking at an early age. Now he's a culinary researcher who's popular
for his simple but serious recipes and his fun presentation.</b>

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:54.610
That's when you serve tempura 
of what's in season.

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:56.320
That's so fancy!

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:57.680
Are we doing tempura today then?

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:00.320
I think I want to do a kakiage today.

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:00.840
I love it!

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:04.050
I want to show you a kakiage that 
you can make without messing up,

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:07.110
<b>Easy Frying Pan
Kakiage</b>

00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:06.370
even if you usually don't make 
any fried foods at home.

00:11:06.370 --> 00:11:07.850
Wonderful. Teach us!

00:11:07.110 --> 00:11:12.090
<b>Easy Frying Pan Kakiage

00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:12.130
Onion, carrot, sakura shrimp. 
Those are all standard ingredients.

00:11:12.120 --> 00:13:09.730
<b>Easy Frying 
Pan Kakiage</b>

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:15.650
With the onion, we just 
cut the root part here.

00:11:15.650 --> 00:11:18.160
Ah, that's how you do it.

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:20.540
Cut them about 5 millimeters thick.

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:21.150
Got it.

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:22.260
The carrot.

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:26.770
First vertically.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:30.190
Then, thinly like this.

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:32.460
Ah, you're making them the 
same size as the onion!

00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:34.150
Add the sakura shrimp.

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:37.580
If you've made kakiage before, 
you probably know about this.

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:39.830
As you fry them, the pot will 
have more and more loose bits.

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:40.450
Yes!

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:42.730
In order to prevent that, there's a trick.

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:43.240
What, really?

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:45.750
Toss the flour onto the ingredients first.

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:46.080
Oh my.

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:49.690
Since I want it to be a bit crispier 
today, I'm adding starch, too.

00:11:49.690 --> 00:11:50.520
Ah-ha!

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:55.230
Mix it so that all the ingredients 
are all covered in the powder.

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:56.340
<b>Thoroughly coat the ingredients 
in the flour and potato starch</b>

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:56.840
So that's the trick!

00:11:56.840 --> 00:12:01.190
Egg yolk and water. Have the 
water as cold as possible.

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:01.640
<b>Beat the egg with cold water</b>

00:12:01.190 --> 00:12:03.290
So make really cold egg water.

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:06.390
Then we add this. Now the two dry 
ingredients will stick together,

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:08.330
and the ingredients won't 
fall apart. Remember this.

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:11.300
This will make that nice crust.

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:13.790
I'm going to use a normal frying pan.

00:12:13.790 --> 00:12:15.180
You can use a frying pan?

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:20.640
Add enough oil so it's 1 centimeter high. 
That's enough to fry kakiage.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:18.450
<b>The oil should be 1 cm high</b>

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:22.100
1 cm of oil is enough!

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:25.090
Wet the tip of a chopstick and put 
it in. If it starts to bubble

00:12:25.090 --> 00:12:29.390
wildly when you put it in, 
it's about 170 degrees.

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:31.200
<b>If the wet tip of a chopstick causes 
bubbling, it's about 170 degrees Celsius</b>

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:31.220
Oh, there it goes, so it's ready now.

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:35.320
Coat the spoon in oil before scooping. 
Then the stuff won't stick to it.

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:36.170
Clever.

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:38.990
Place them in the oil.

00:12:39.420 --> 00:12:43.130
With the regular way, doing this would 
cause the stuff to already fall apart.

00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:45.270
But with today's method, 
that's unlikely to happen.

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:47.800
That's because the ingredients 
are coated really well.

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:51.470
Once these are in, stab them with 
chopsticks a bit and loosen them a bit.

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:52.320
Roger.

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:55.630
It's been two minutes, so we flip these now.

00:12:55.630 --> 00:12:56.290
Right.

00:12:56.290 --> 00:12:58.350
Once you've flipped them, 
stab them once more.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:12:58.900
Ah-ha.

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:01.300
At the end, we're going to raise the 
temperature of the oil really high

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:04.730
<b>Raise the temperature of the oil at the end to push out the moisture and oil from the batter</b>

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:03.290
to drive the moisture and 
oil out of the batter.

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:04.750
That'll give us the
nice crispy finish.

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:06.080
Let's take them out.

00:13:07.030 --> 00:13:09.730
They are really fried 
properly with that little oil!

00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:12.760
Even for people for whom the hurdles 
of making tempura at home are too high,

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:19.940
<b>Onion and sakura shrimp kakiage</b>

00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:16.140
this method of making kakiage 
should be easy to follow.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:18.360
These are good on soba 
or other noodles, too.

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:22.670
They go well with beer, sake, and 
shochu, so please give this a try.

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:24.970
Then I'm going to have a bite.

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:33.020
<b>Today's high-speed footage</b>

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:28.760
Crispy! The veggies' sweetness and the 
sakura shrimp's flavors are melding!

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:31.610
Easy to make in a frying pan. 
Please try making this!

